📊 Tables in Excel & Power BI — Data Analytics Learning
📌 Today's Learning

Today, I explored one of the fundamental concepts in Data Analytics and Business Intelligence — Tables.
Tables may look like simple rows and columns, but they form the foundation for organizing, cleaning, analyzing, modeling, and visualizing data.
The main learning was understanding how tables work differently in Microsoft Excel and Power BI, along with PivotTables, Conditional Formatting, Raw Tables, summarized data, and real-world applications.
---
🔹 What is a Table?

A table is a structured collection of data organized into:

* Rows → Individual records or observations
* Columns → Attributes or fields
* Headers → Names describing each column
* Cells → Individual values

Example:

| Order ID | Product | Category    |  Sales |
| -------- | ------- | ----------- | -----: |
| 1001     | Laptop  | Electronics | $1,200 |
| 1002     | Mouse   | Accessories |    $25 |
| 1003     | Chair   | Furniture   |   $150 |

Structured tables make data easier to filter, sort, clean, calculate, analyze, and visualize.
---
🟢 Tables in Excel

Excel Tables help convert ordinary cell ranges into structured datasets.

Key Features

* Sorting and filtering
* Structured references
* Automatic formatting
* Calculated columns
* Automatic expansion when new records are added
* Easy integration with PivotTables and charts
* Faster management of small-to-medium datasets

Excel Tables are especially useful for quick analysis, operational reports, financial calculations, and everyday data management.
---
🟡 Tables in Power BI

In Power BI, tables are an important part of the **data model**.

Data can be imported from sources such as Excel, CSV, SQL databases, APIs, and cloud platforms.

Tables can then be transformed, connected, and analyzed.

Power BI Tables Enable:

* Data modeling
* Relationships between datasets
* DAX calculations
* Measures and calculated columns
* Filtering across related tables
* Interactive reports
* KPI development
* Dashboard visualizations

Example:

```text
Customers
    │
    │ CustomerID
    ↓
Sales
    │
    │ ProductID
    ↓
Products

Instead of keeping everything in one large table, Power BI can connect multiple related tables to create an efficient analytical model.
---
🔄 Raw Table vs PivotTable

# Raw Table

A Raw Table contains detailed records.

Example:
text
Order | Product | Region | Sales
1001  | Laptop  | East   | 1200
1002  | Mouse   | West   | 25
1003  | Laptop  | East   | 1400

Each row represents an individual transaction.

# PivotTable

A PivotTable summarizes the detailed data.

Example:

| Region | Total Sales |
| ------ | ----------: |
| East   |      $2,600 |
| West   |         $25 |

# Simple Difference

Raw Table → Stores detailed data
PivotTable → Summarizes data for analysis
PivotTables allow analysts to quickly calculate:
* SUM
* COUNT
* AVERAGE
* MIN/MAX
* Category totals
* Regional performance
* Monthly trends
---
🎨 Conditional Formatting:
Conditional Formatting visually highlights values based on predefined conditions.

For example:
text
Sales Performance

$15,000  🟢 High
$8,000   🟡 Medium
$2,000   🔴 Low
It helps analysts quickly identify:
* High and low performers
* Trends
* Exceptions
* Outliers
* Important KPIs
* Values requiring attention

This makes tables easier to interpret without manually examining every value.
---
⚖️ Excel Tables vs Power BI Tables

| Excel               | Power BI               |
| ------------------- | ---------------------- |
| Quick data analysis | Business Intelligence  |
| Formulas            | DAX                    |
| Filters & sorting   | Advanced filtering     |
| PivotTables         | Interactive visuals    |
| Smaller datasets    | Larger datasets        |
| Basic relationships | Advanced data models   |
| Individual reports  | Interactive dashboards |

Excel is excellent for flexible, quick analysis, while Power BI becomes powerful when we need **data modeling, scalable analysis, interactive visualization, and business reporting**.

They can also work together as part of the same analytics workflow.
